================================================================================
 SimulIDE Code Editor - Syntax Highlighting File Reference
================================================================================
 File type : .syntax
 Location : resources/data/codeeditor/syntax/
 Loaded by: Highlighter::readSyntaxFile()  (src/gui/editorwidget/highlighter.cpp)

A .syntax file is a plain text file that tells the SimulIDE editor how to
color the source code of a given language. Each file defines a set of
"rules" (e.g. keywords, numbers, comments, strings). The editor applies the
rules line by line, painting matching text with the colors and styles you
declare.

This file is a comment/cookbook only. It is not parsed as a syntax file.
Use "#" freely at the start of any line for your own notes -- the parser
simply ignores lines that do not match a rule definition.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. OVERALL STRUCTURE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A syntax file has three kinds of lines:

  1. A rule list header   (required, must be near the top)
  2. A "-style:" line     (defines the colors/style for one rule)
  3. A "rule:" line       (lists the words or RegExps that rule matches)

A file can declare MANY rules. The order of the rules matters (see section 6).
Blank lines and "#" comment lines are ignored.

Layout template:

    # Comment lines (optional)
    rules: ruleA ruleB ruleC

    ruleA-style: <fgColor> <bgColor> <bold> <italic>
    ruleA: <pattern> <pattern> ...

    ruleB-style: <fgColor> <bgColor> <bold> <italic>
    ruleB: <pattern> <pattern> ...

    ... and so on ...


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 2. THE "rules:" HEADER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The "rules:" line lists every rule this file defines, in the order you want
them, separated by spaces.

    rules: data keyword1 keyword2 lineComment

This tells the parser which rule names to look for. Any rule you define in
the "-style:"/"rule:" pairs below must also be named here (The two files
naming them are normally the same). Rules are matched and applied in the
order they appear in this list.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 3. THE "-style:" LINE  (visual appearance)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
For every rule you must describe how matched text looks:

    <ruleName>-style: <foreground> <background> <bold> <italic>

    ruleName     - must be one of the names listed in "rules:".
    foreground   - text color:  default  or  #RRGGBB
    background   - fill color:  default  or  #RRGGBB
    bold         - true | false
    italic       - true | false

Using "default" for a color leaves that channel unchanged (handy when you
only want, say, a background highlight and nothing else).

Example:

    keyword-style: #202060 default true false
    #              ^foreground ^background ^bold ^italic
    #              (dark blue)  (left alone) (bold)(not italic)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 4. THE "rule:" LINE  (what gets matched)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The rule line holds one or more patterns separated by spaces. A pattern is
either:

   (a) a PLAIN WORD  -> matched as a whole keyword
   (b) a QUOTED RegExp -> matched as a regular expression

Example mixing both in one rule line:

    number: 255 128 \b[0-9]+ \b0[xX][0-9a-fA-F]+

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 4.1 Plain words (keywords / identifiers)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A token that does NOT start with a double-quote is treated as a literal word.
The editor automatically wraps it with word boundaries (\b), so it only
matches the whole word and not text inside longer words.

    data: uint8_t uint16_t int long char void

    # "uint8_t" is highlighted only when it appears as a whole word.
    # It will NOT match the "uint8_t" inside "my_uint8_t_count".

Plain keywords are also collected into the editor autocomplete/completion
list automatically (see CodeEditor::addKeyWords).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 4.2 Quoted RegExp patterns
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A token that STARTS with a double-quote is taken as a raw Qt/QRegularExpression.
The surrounding quotes are stripped before use. Quotes do NOT need to be
closed specially -- but for valid signatures enclose each start and end
with " and ".

    quotation: "\"(\\.|[^\"])*\"" "\'(\\.|[^\'])*\'"

Quoted RegExps are NOT added to the autocomplete list (they are not words).
Note: inside the .syntax file, backslashes that appear inside a quoted
pattern are read literally, so to build a \b or \w in the final regex you
write it normally as it appears.

Example (uppercase hex numbers):

    hexNumber: "\b0[xX][0-9a-fA-F]+\b"

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 4.3 Escaping inside quoted RegExps
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Remember that a "." , "*" , "+" , "(" etc. are special RegExp characters.
To match them literally you must escape them with a backslash (\\ inside the
quoted string). Some useful idioms:

    Line-end anchor  : "\...$"
    Column start     : "^..."                      (matches at line start)
    Word boundary    : "\bword\b"
    Non-digit        : "\D+"

Because the pattern is quoted, a literal backslash is written as "\\\\"
in the file so the parser stores a single "\".


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5. THE SPECIAL "multiLineComment" RULE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Most rules only match within a single line. To color a comment that can
span several lines (for example C-style /* ... */ ) you must use a rule
named EXACTLY "multiLineComment". It takes exactly TWO quoted RegExps:
the start delimiter and the end delimiter.

    multiLineComment-style: #00a651 default false true
    multiLineComment: "/\\*" "\\*/"

How it behaves:
   - Matches from the START marker to the END marker.
   - If the END marker is not found on the same line, the highlight carries
     over to the following lines until the END marker is found.
   - This is the only built-in rule that spans multiple lines.

You should generally name a plain single-line comment rule differently
(e.g. "lineComment") instead of reusing this one for single-line comments.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6. RULE ORDERING / OVERLAY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The editor applies rules one after another, painting over previous matches.
Later rules / later matches are drawn ON TOP of earlier ones. If two patterns
match the same text, the last applied one wins.

Because of this, put the broadest or least specific rules first and the most
specific rules last if you need a particular one to win. Priority follows the
order in the "rules:" header.

Also note that rules read from the file are matched case-insensitively.
(Device object/member/type rules injected by the simulator at runtime are
matched case-sensitively, but that is handled internally -- you do not need
to worry about it when writing a .syntax file.)


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 7. COLORS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Colors are in HTML/CSS hex form: #RRGGBB

    Black   : #000000
    White   : #FFFFFF
    Red     : #FF0000
    Green   : #00A651
    Blue    : #0000FF

Use "default" to leave the foreground or background color untouched. This is
useful for column-oriented files where you only want to tint the background.

Dark themes: if the current theme is dark, the editor first looks for a file
with the same name but "_dark.syntax" (e.g. cpp_dark.syntax). If that file
exists it is used as-is; otherwise the normal colors are used with their
lightness inverted automatically as a fallback.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 8. AVAILABLE OPTIONS (QUICK REFERENCE)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Colors : default | #RRGGBB
    Bold   : true | false
    Italic : true | false

    Pattern kinds:
        Plain word   -> whole-word keyword (auto \b...\b), added to completion
        Quoted RegExp->, raw regular expression, not added to completion

    Special rule: multiLineComment  -> "<startRegExp>" "<endRegExp>"


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 9. MINIMAL WORKING EXAMPLE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Below is a complete, tiny syntax file you can copy and extend.

    # --- tiny.syntax -----------------------------------------------
    rules: comment keyword number string

    comment-style: #646464 default false true
    comment: "#[^\n]*"

    keyword-style: #202060 default true false
    keyword: for if else while return

    number-style: #3030B8 default false false
    number: "\b[0-9]+\b"

    string-style: #206410 default false false
    string: "\"(\\.|[^\"])*\""
    # -----------------------------------------------------------------

For a broader set of examples, look at the shipped files in this folder
(cpp.syntax, xml.syntax, hex.syntax, gcbasic.syntax, etc.).
